.TH PCIX 4 "2026-10-09" "Substrate" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
pcix \- IBM PC/IX (System III for the IBM PC) execution personality
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B PC/IX
personality
.RB ( PERS_PCIX )
runs the programs of IBM Personal Computer Interactive Executive 1.0, the
UNIX System III that INTERACTIVE Systems made for the IBM PC XT in 1984.
It is rooted at
.IR /perso/pcix ,
where a program's absolute paths are looked for first.
.PP
PC/IX is the system one release before the one the 16-bit half of the
Xenix personality runs: Xenix/286 numbers its system calls as System V
does, System V as System III did, and the structures the calls fill in
are the same.
A PC/IX call is therefore run by the Xenix/286 implementation of it.
What this personality has of its own is how a call arrives and what a
few calls do with their arguments, all of which the distribution
documents in
.IR /usr/include/sys.s .
.SS Executable format
.B struct exec
of PC/IX's
.IR <a.out.h> :
magic bytes 01 03, then
.BR a_flags ,
.B a_cpu
(0x04, the 8086) and the length of the header.
MINIX took this format from PC/IX and ELKS has it from MINIX, and the
ELKS loader loads all three.
A file is a PC/IX program if its flags are 0x70 (executable, separate
I&D, pure text), which is how PC/IX's linker marks everything it ships
and no ELKS program is marked; or, for a program linked without
.BR \-n ,
if a PC/IX process executes it or it is named under
.IR /perso/pcix .
.PP
A program has one text segment and one data segment, or one segment for
both, reached through the process's local descriptor table.
The data segment is the full 64K whatever
.B a_misc
asks for, with the arguments at the top of it:
on entry the stack pointer addresses
.IR argc ,
followed by the argument pointers, a null, the environment pointers and
a null.
There is no
.BR brk (2);
the C library divides the segment between heap and stack itself.
.SS System calls
Call
.I N
is the instruction
.BR "int 0x80+N" ,
a vector for each call and no number in a register.
The arguments are on the stack as for a C function, below a word where
its return address would be.
The result is in AX, or DX:AX for a long; the carry flag set means AX
holds an error number.
No other register changes.
.PP
The calls are System III's, by their numbers there.
Those that differ from the Xenix/286 call of the same number:
.TP
.B fork
The child continues at the instruction after the
.BR int ,
the parent two bytes further on.
.TP
.B wait
Takes the address at which to store the status, and a second address
(for the dead child's unused memory, which is not recorded).
.TP
.BR getpid ", " getuid ", " getgid ", " pipe
The second value (parent, effective id, write end) is returned in DX.
.TP
.BR time ", " stime
.B time
takes no argument and
.B stime
takes the time itself, not its address.
.TP
.B setpgrp
One call for
.BR getpgrp (2)
and
.BR setpgrp (2),
its argument 0 or 1.
.TP
.B utssys
The function is the third argument; 0 is
.BR uname (2),
which reports
.B PC/IX
release 1.0 on an
.BR ibmpc .
Function 2 is
.BR ustat (2):
the free blocks and inodes of the filesystem whose device number is the
.I st_dev
that
.BR stat (2)
reported.
.TP
.B lockf
System III's record lock on bytes of a file from the descriptor's
position:
.B F_ULOCK
unlocks,
.B F_LOCK
waits and
.B F_TLOCK
fails with
.B EACCES
if another process holds any of them.  They are
.BR fcntl (2)'s
exclusive record locks, and go when the file is closed.
.TP
.B profil
Accepted; no samples are taken, so a program built for profiling runs and
writes a
.I mon.out
of zeros.
.PP
The Xenix extensions, the System V IPC calls,
.BR brk ,
.BR ptrace ,
.B halt
and
.B inuinfo
fail with
.BR EINVAL .
.SS Floating point
PC/IX's compiler writes every 8087 instruction with
.B INT
where its
.B WAIT
prefix belongs
.RB ( "CD D9 E0"
for
.BR "9B D9 E0" ),
so that a kernel on a machine without the coprocessor can emulate it from
the trap and one with it can put the prefix back.  The personality puts
it back, in the process's own copy of its text, and the instruction runs
on the processor's floating-point unit.
.SS Signals
A handler is entered with the signal number, the flags and the
interrupted instruction pointer pushed, the number on top, and every
register as it was; it returns with
.B popf
and a near
.BR ret ,
which is what the C library's own stub around a handler does.
As in System III the disposition is reset to the default before the
handler runs and no signal is blocked while it does.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /perso/pcix
The PC/IX tree.
.I tools/pcix/build-image.sh
builds an image of it from the distribution floppies.
.TP
.I /dev
The kernel's, not the tree's.  The nodes in
.I /perso/pcix/dev
are PC/IX's own, numbered for its drivers
.RI ( /dev/null
is 4,2), and are never opened: a PC/IX program that names
.I /dev/null
or
.I /dev/tty
gets substrate's.
.SH NOTES
Enable tracing of the calls with
.B debug=perso:pcix:syscall
on the kernel command line.
.PP
.BR ps (1)
and
.BR df (1)
of PC/IX read kernel memory and the raw disk, and report errors.
.PP
A shell script is run as System III runs one:
.BR exece (2)
fails with
.B ENOEXEC
and the shell reads the file itself.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR svr3 (4),
.BR svr4 (4),
.BR sunos (4)
